#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129073 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b02082a8-b56e-5478-859d-51db7273f765" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/id #uuid "066f76be-83f6-52fd-ab3f-00786d18f64e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9866" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx = t-\\sin t, \\quad y = 1-\\cos t, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,\\pi\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=\\pi.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\sin^2 t +\\cos^2 t= 1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129074 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129319 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a9a59d4-e8c8-59ac-bf23-eb70254a7661" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/id #uuid "b7f404a3-8885-5a33-9cfb-1e12a6c745a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-9867" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx = \\sin t, \\quad y = e^t, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,1\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129075 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129320 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7087df58-f97e-5423-813a-9aada16ee12a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/id #uuid "205f4d6b-affa-530e-979b-a394e7a46bd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9869" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx = 1+3t, \\quad y = 2t^{3/2}, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,1\\right].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)=3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3/2})=3{t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{{(3)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{9+9t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{9(1+t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\cdot 3\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u = 1+t.$ Then, $1+3t=3u-2$ and $\\textrm{d}u = \\textrm d t.$ We also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}(3u-2)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}(3{u}^{3/2}-2{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{6{u}^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{4{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =6\\pi [(\\frac{6({2}^{5/2})}{5}-\\frac{4({2}^{3/2})}{3})-(\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{4}{3})] \\\\  & =6\\pi [(\\frac{24\\sqrt{2}}{5}-\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{4}{3})] \\\\  & =6\\pi [\\frac{32\\sqrt{2}}{15}+\\frac{2}{15}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{5}(16\\sqrt{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129320 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7087df58-f97e-5423-813a-9aada16ee12a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c3ac195-5a28-5c11-99ed-a06a6384cb26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435091} {:answer/id #uuid "206381c1-a55a-5bbf-8129-7ade3cea9753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435094} {:answer/id #uuid "333420d8-3841-5986-a880-12239e3b0c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435098} {:answer/id #uuid "867968e5-1470-5d6b-bbb2-c22bb39822ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435104} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4dc2f2-4b7b-5c53-9837-433249177feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f4dc2f2-4b7b-5c53-9837-433249177feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435105} :answer-field/id #uuid "f67f1dc2-47fa-5fa4-a382-f69736f1f021" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d124471f-78aa-50f9-bfd3-0eadf2ea5bb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-138972" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}={sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}),$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\cdot |2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\pi $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{0}-2(1-{u}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =16\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-{u}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =16\\pi \\cdot {[u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =16\\pi (1-\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =16\\pi (\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129319 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a9a59d4-e8c8-59ac-bf23-eb70254a7661" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c721d8c-9e46-58a3-95bb-3174862c4a01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435093} {:answer/id #uuid "5f781be2-8bd2-520a-9a09-5f6c042226c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435102} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc1610f-6e04-5f46-8bfb-9596dcf2e267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435113} {:answer/id #uuid "df954cef-dd13-5a8f-ac48-dd0715a02bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435114} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e77fce-c9b3-50a6-aca4-e69c1a78f228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df954cef-dd13-5a8f-ac48-dd0715a02bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435114} :answer-field/id #uuid "61ae82f2-6c7c-57cb-9b66-5fd5901e4df5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a5498dd-6e1c-5317-8d48-505ce94b33dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-139054" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129319 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a9a59d4-e8c8-59ac-bf23-eb70254a7661" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25838e3e-57a1-5fd9-8feb-f77678c5ccc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t(t+1)\\sqrt{1+6{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435096} {:answer/id #uuid "29c95824-df17-58a2-8aac-76df26add78b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+36{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435097} {:answer/id #uuid "4b3a2be8-e4d5-5828-9be5-65cc4722dd19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435099} {:answer/id #uuid "579fa76c-1f64-56b5-b97c-4581e9fe53fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+1)\\sqrt{1+36{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435101} {:answer/id #uuid "95766dce-20ec-56a7-bf74-a65bab0ccb22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+1){t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "579fa76c-1f64-56b5-b97c-4581e9fe53fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+1)\\sqrt{1+36{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435101} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffdc95ce-c8de-554f-af86-018019927d83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "969f9c5c-90f0-5451-a64e-6e16b1103b2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-139055" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=t+1,\\,y=2{t}^{3},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})=6{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+1)\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(6{t}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+1)\\sqrt{1+36{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b02082a8-b56e-5478-859d-51db7273f765" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1889d211-580a-525d-8863-d0bc666144a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(5t+2)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435092} {:answer/id #uuid "c1dbc8d7-24ce-50f6-8164-8b4e61b2fccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435109} {:answer/id #uuid "d33dff8a-de5a-5820-bbc2-521bfdade558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t(3t+2)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435111} {:answer/id #uuid "d855760e-bbf6-5e09-b377-fb92b66bdfbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(3t+2)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435112} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1ce31b-4cfb-5eab-8d4f-6c08ab46d8c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}2t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d855760e-bbf6-5e09-b377-fb92b66bdfbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(3t+2)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435112} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ebb6a09-3962-51d8-a359-86c686b5cd13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "310c6308-d59f-5c23-bf3f-94f16264b626" :question/math-academy-id "q-139066" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,y=3t+2,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)=2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(3t+2)\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{(3)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(3t+2)\\sqrt{13}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{13}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(3t+2)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b02082a8-b56e-5478-859d-51db7273f765" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217edb44-0fc7-5d6d-964e-6c05dca7cb50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}+{2}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435095} {:answer/id #uuid "53eb5f8d-2c90-56b2-af3c-af00df5e8365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}+{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435100} {:answer/id #uuid "97765c6f-bd17-5d3b-a5c6-230f3b86329e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{t}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435107} {:answer/id #uuid "aa6427b7-b0e6-5e33-9aca-612cfab836af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{3t}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435108} {:answer/id #uuid "c63274df-6f1f-56f7-bcf0-d35e3a9b440e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c63274df-6f1f-56f7-bcf0-d35e3a9b440e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435110} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5dd24f4-4688-5465-a47d-3059c114e3fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "048f2885-159c-5d24-83a6-9ee301309f6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-139070" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{3},\\,y={2}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t})={2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}+{({2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+{2}^{2t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435127 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129320 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7087df58-f97e-5423-813a-9aada16ee12a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "717f0fc3-0cb4-5e82-a464-045bfd255128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "717f0fc3-0cb4-5e82-a464-045bfd255128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435103} :answer-field/id #uuid "019c2510-13c6-5cb5-adc7-f6823183b11a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b3bc80a6-ca15-5095-bc92-205f586735f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-352092" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{t}{4},\\,t\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[{e}^{t}]={e}^{t}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{t}{4}]={e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{[{e}^{t}]}^{2}+{[{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{4}]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{4t}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{16}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{16}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recognizing that the term under the square root is a perfect square, we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}({e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}({e}^{3t}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}({e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u={e}^{t}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$  We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}({e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({u}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{u}{4}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{2}{4})-(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4})] \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}] \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{28}{12}+\\frac{3}{12}] \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{31}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{31\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
